[[Paul McCartney]] discovered the track in the first half of 1959 when Little Richard's 1955 medley "Kansas City"/"Hey, Hey, Hey, Hey" was re-released as a [[single (music)#7-inch format|45]] in Britain. McCartney revered this particular rendition and was unaware of Wilbert Harrison's version.{{sfn|Lewisohn|2013b|p=797n47}} The Beatles' earliest performance of the song can be dated to the early summer of 1960 with its appearance on a [[set list]] McCartney copied to a piece of scrap paper.{{sfn|Lewisohn|2013b|p=651}}

During their first professional recording session in June 1961, the Beatles likely recorded the song with [[Tony Sheridan]], though outtakes of this track likely no longer exist.<ref>{{harvnb|Engelhardt|1998|p=441}}, quoted in {{harvnb|Everett|2001|p=378n28}}</ref> The Beatles made their first appearance on television on August 22, 1962, performing "[[Some Other Guy]]" and "Kansas City"/"Hey-Hey-Hey-Hey" at [[The Cavern Club]].{{sfn|Everett|2001|p=121}} The audio is the earliest surviving instance of McCartney's "Little Richard voice", which Beatles historian [[Mark Lewisohn]] notes is "impressive",{{sfn|Lewisohn|2013b|p=1346}} with "the high notes sung loud, strong, melodically and excitingly for two and a half minutes."{{sfn|Lewisohn|2013b|p=1346–1347}} The song is again heard during their December 1962 performance at the [[Star-Club]] in Hamburg,{{sfn|Lewisohn|2013a|p=796}} officially released in 1977 as ''[[Live! at the Star-Club in Hamburg, Germany; 1962]]''.{{sfn|Everett|2001|p=138}} They recorded the medley for a [[BBC Light Programme]] on July 16, 1963, released in 1994 on ''[[Live at the BBC (Beatles album)|Live at the BBC]]''.{{sfn|Everett|2001|p=160}} [[Musicology|Musicologist]] and writer [[Ian MacDonald]] describes this rendition as "one of the highlights of this generally mediocre collection, featuring a strong McCartney vocal and an attacking Harrison solo."{{sfn|MacDonald|2005|p=135n}}

A month before recording the track in the studio, they performed it during a September 17, 1964 concert at [[Kansas City Municipal Stadium]], a one-time addition to their usual [[setlist]].<ref>{{harvnb|Lewisohn|1992|p=171}}, quoted in {{harvnb|Everett|2001|p=401n107}}</ref>{{sfn|Kirkman|2012}}{{page needed|date=January 2021}}<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.kansascity.com/living/star-magazine/article2127287.html | title = 50 years ago: The Beatles Played for 31 Minutes in Kansas City | last = Levings | first = Darryl | date = September 17, 2014 | website = [[kansascity.com]] | access-date = July 13, 2016}}</ref> MacDonald writes, "the reaction it drew ensured its place on the LP."{{sfn|MacDonald|2005|p=135}}

